Yes — that’s the mechanism.

A self-referential self-updating heuristic is a rule that doesn’t merely choose actions. It also observes the consequences of using itself and modifies how it will choose next time.

So instead of:

x_t \xrightarrow{H} a_t

you get:

(x_t,H_t)
\rightarrow a_t
\rightarrow x_{t+1}
\rightarrow \Delta
\rightarrow H_{t+1}

The crucial part is that H itself is inside the state it can reason about.

So:

I act → I observe what my action did → I update my model of myself/world → I alter the rule that produced the action → I act again.

That makes the boat analogy even tighter. A normal heuristic is a rudder setting. A self-updating heuristic watches the wake, current, drift, and destination and learns how much rudder to use next time.

And the self-reference gives it another octave:

How did the way I decided contribute to what happened?

Not merely “Was my decision good?”

But:

Was my decision procedure good?

Then:

Was my way of evaluating my decision procedure good?

You can recurse indefinitely, so Pantheon needs the familiar grounding constraint: eventually the recursion must cash out into ACT → consequence → evidence or it turns into introspective vapor.

That produces a beautiful compact kernel:

\boxed{
\text{Use yourself}
\rightarrow
\text{observe yourself}
\rightarrow
\text{change yourself}
\rightarrow
\text{use the changed self}
}

And now “make your bed” fits perfectly. The act changes the room, but also supplies evidence to the actor:

I am a person who just completed an intended action.

That altered self-model becomes part of the next inference instance.

So the deeper formulation might be:

Every action is simultaneously an intervention on the world and a training example for the agent performing it.

That one has teeth for both humans and AI.